= 1. The South African Crucible and Return to India (1915–1918)

Mahatma Gandhi's leadership of the Indian national struggle was shaped by two decades of experimental activism in South Africa, which historian Chandran Devanesan famously characterized as "the making of the Mahatma":

== The South African Laboratory (1893–1914)
In South Africa, Gandhi went as a legal counsel for a Gujarati merchant firm, but stayed for twenty-one years fighting racial discrimination (*apartheid*):
- *Satyagraha Formulation:* He forged the philosophical doctrine of *Satyagraha* (truth-force or soul-force) based on unwavering non-violence (*ahimsa*), civil disobedience, and voluntary suffering.
- *Communal Harmony:* He welded together a diverse diaspora of Indians—Hindus, Muslims, Parsis, Christians, upper-caste merchants, and low-caste Tamil indentured laborers.
- *Intellectual Influences:* Gandhi's economic and spiritual worldview was profoundly transformed by three thinkers:
  - *John Ruskin's Unto This Last:* Translated into Gujarati as *Sarvodaya* ("Universal Uplift"), inspiring the establishment of the *Phoenix Settlement* (1904) near Durban and the *Tolstoy Farm* (1910) near Johannesburg, where all community members engaged in equal manual labor.
  - *Leo Tolstoy's The Kingdom of God Is Within You:* Cultivating radical Christian anarchism and non-violent resistance.
  - *Henry David Thoreau's Essay on Civil Disobedience:* Inspiring the refusal to obey unjust, immoral laws.

== Return to India and the BHU Speech (1915–1916)
In *January 1915*, Gandhi returned permanently to India:
- *Gokhale's Mentorship:* On the advice of his political mentor, *Gopal Krishna Gokhale*, Gandhi spent his first year traveling by third-class railway across the subcontinent to observe the realities of colonial exploitation firsthand.
- *The BHU Speech (February 1916):* Gandhi's first major public appearance occurred at the opening ceremony of the *Banaras Hindu University (BHU)* in February 1916. In a speech that shocked the assembled elite, Gandhi fiercely rebuked the diamond-studded princes and wealthy lawyers:
  - *"There is no salvation for India unless you strip yourselves of this jewelry and hold it in trust for your countrymen in India."*
  - He declared that nationalism could have no real meaning as long as the vast mass of impoverished peasantry remained voiceless and exploited.

== The Triad of Local Satyagrahas (1917–1918)
Gandhi established his moral authority through three localized agrarian and labor interventions:
1. *Champaran Satyagraha (1917, Bihar):* Persuaded by an unlettered indigo cultivator, *Raj Kumar Shukla*, Gandhi traveled to Champaran to investigate the oppressive *tinkathia* system (which legally forced peasants to cultivate indigo on 3/20th of their holdings). Despite colonial orders to leave, Gandhi refused, conducted extensive public inquiries, and compelled the government to abolish tinkathia and refund 25% of illegal extractions.
2. *Ahmedabad Mill Strike (February–March 1918, Gujarat):* Intervening in a wage dispute between textile mill owners and workers over the withdrawal of the "plague bonus", Gandhi undertook his *first fast unto death*. Supported by *Anasuya Sarabhai*, he secured an arbitration award granting workers a *35 percent wage increase*.
3. *Kheda Satyagraha (March–June 1918, Gujarat):* When severe drought caused crops to fail below one-fourth of normal yield, peasants were legally entitled to revenue remission under the Land Revenue Code. When colonial authorities refused, Gandhi organized peasants to withhold taxes peacefully. Guided by *Vallabhbhai Patel*, the government eventually issued secret orders exempting poor peasants who could not pay.

= 2. The Pan-Indian Surge: Rowlatt, Jallianwala Bagh & Khilafat

The conclusion of World War I brought severe economic distress, inflation, and coercive wartime measures that set the stage for a nationwide confrontation:

#figure(
  image("assets/hist_gandhi_satyagraha_quadrant.png", width: 84%),
  caption: [Figure 1 — The Strategic Quadrant of Gandhian Satyagraha: Harmonizing moral philosophy, non-violent mass defiance, constructive rural work, and institutional negotiation.],
)

== The Rowlatt Act and Nationwide Satyagraha (1919)
In March 1919, the imperial government passed the *Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act* (popularly known as the *Rowlatt Act*), based on the recommendations of Sir Sidney Rowlatt:
- *Coercive Provisions:* The act authorized the detention of any political suspect for up to two years without warrant, trial, or legal representation (*"No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal"*).
- *The Nationwide Hartal (6 April 1919):* Gandhi organized the *Rowlatt Satyagraha Sabha*, calling for a nationwide general strike (*hartal*) on *6 April 1919*. Business closed across the country, transforming anti-colonial protest from an elite constitutional debate into a truly pan-Indian popular movement.

== The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April 1919)
The movement encountered savage imperial counter-violence in Punjab:
- *The Gathering:* On *13 April 1919* (the festival of Baisakhi), a peaceful crowd of thousands gathered in the enclosed compound of *Jallianwala Bagh* in Amritsar to celebrate the harvest festival and protest the arrest of local leaders Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satyapal.
- *The Slaughter:* Brigadier-General *Reginald Dyer* blocked the sole narrow exit with troops and, without issuing any warning, ordered his soldiers to fire continuously into the trapped crowd until ammunition was exhausted. Hundreds of men, women, and children were butchered.
- *Renunciation of Honours:* In overwhelming anguish and moral outrage, *Rabindranath Tagore* renounced his British *Knighthood*, declaring that the time had come when badges of honor made shame conspicuous.

== The Khilafat Alliance (1919–1920)
Simultaneously, Indian Muslims were deeply agitated over the harsh treatment meted out to the Ottoman Caliph (Khalifa) by victorious European powers following the dismemberment of the Ottoman Empire in the Treaty of Sevres:
- *The Ali Brothers:* *Muhammad Ali* and *Shaukat Ali* founded the *All India Khilafat Committee*.
- *Strategic Coalition:* Gandhi recognized a historic opportunity to forge an unbreakable alliance between Hindus and Muslims: *"An opportunity of uniting Hindus and Mohammedans as would not arise in a hundred years."* He persuaded the Indian National Congress to endorse the Khilafat cause and launch Non-Cooperation.

= 3. The Non-Cooperation Movement and Its Aftermath (1920–1922)

The Non-Cooperation Movement was officially sanctioned at the special Calcutta Congress session (September 1920) and ratified at Nagpur (December 1920):

== The Program of Non-Violent Non-Cooperation
Gandhi argued that the British Empire existed only because Indians cooperated with it; if Indians refused to cooperate, British rule would collapse within a year (*"Swaraj in one year"*):
- *Boycott Program:* Surrender of imperial titles and honorary offices (Gandhi returned his *Kaiser-i-Hind* gold medal); boycott of government law courts, schools, colleges, and legislative councils; boycott of foreign cloth and British manufactured commodities.
- *Constructive Program:* Promotion of hand-spun and hand-woven *khadi*, universal popularization of the *charkha* (spinning wheel), establishment of national educational institutions (*Kashi Vidyapeeth*, *Gujarat Vidyapith*, *Jamia Millia Islamia*), promotion of Hindu-Muslim unity, and abolition of the curse of untouchability.

== Subaltern Upsurge and Varied Understandings of Swaraj
As the movement spread across India, ordinary subaltern peasants and workers interpreted "Swaraj" through their own local needs and cultural idioms:
- *Peasant Resistance in Awadh:* Led by *Baba Ramchandra*, Awadh peasants refused to pay illegal cesses (*abwabs*) and demanded security of tenure against oppressive taluqdars.
- *Tribal Forest Satyagrahas:* In the Gudem Hills of Andhra Pradesh, tribal leader *Alluri Sitarama Raju* led an armed guerrilla struggle against oppressive colonial forest laws, claiming inspiration from Gandhi while defending tribal rights to shifting cultivation.
- *Labor Strikes:* In Assam, tea garden laborers struck work, marched along railway tracks, and demanded living wages, singing that Gandhi Maharaj had freed them from European tea planters.

== Chauri Chaura and the Historic Trial (1922)
Just as the movement was approaching its climax, it erupted into violence:
- *The Incident (4 February 1922):* At *Chauri Chaura* in the Gorakhpur district of Uttar Pradesh, a peaceful procession of peasants was fired upon by the police. An enraged mob retaliated by locking the police station and setting it on fire, killing *22 policemen*.
- *The Abrupt Withdrawal:* Deeply committed to absolute non-violence, Gandhi declared that the nation was not yet spiritually disciplined for true Satyagraha. Overriding fierce protests from senior colleagues (Subhas Chandra Bose, Jawaharlal Nehru, and C.R. Das), Gandhi unilaterally called off the movement through the *Bardoli Resolution* on *12 February 1922*.
- *The Broomfield Trial (March 1922):* Gandhi was arrested in March 1922 and tried before British Judge *C.N. Broomfield* on charges of sedition. In a historic speech, Gandhi declared: *"I am here, therefore, to invite and cheerfully submit to the highest penalty that can be inflicted upon me for what in law is a deliberate crime, and what appears to me to be the highest duty of a citizen."* Judge Broomfield sentenced him to six years' imprisonment, remarking on Gandhi's noble character.

= 4. Constructive Work, Simon Commission & Purna Swaraj (1923–1929)

Following Gandhi's early release on medical grounds in 1924, Indian nationalism entered a phase of internal debate and grassroots reconstruction:

== Swarajists vs. No-Changers
A deep tactical divide fractured the Indian National Congress:
- *Swarajists:* Led by *Chitta Ranjan (C.R.) Das* and *Motilal Nehru*, they formed the *Congress-Khilafat Swarajya Party* (1923). They advocated entering the legislative councils under the Government of India Act 1919 to "wreck the reforms from within" through continuous obstruction.
- *No-Changers:* Led by *Vallabhbhai Patel*, *Rajendra Prasad*, and *C. Rajagopalachari*, they opposed council entry and urged full dedication to Gandhi's constructive program—khadi spinning, village sanitation, national education, and anti-untouchability campaigns.

== The Simon Commission and the Nehru Report (1927–1928)
In November 1927, the British government appointed an Indian Statutory Commission under Sir John Simon to review the constitutional working of the 1919 Act:
- *The All-White Insult:* The *Simon Commission* did not include a single Indian member. When the commission landed in Bombay in February 1928, it was greeted with black flags, nationwide general strikes, and the thunderous slogan: *"Simon Go Back!"*
- *Lajpat Rai's Martyrdom:* In October 1928, during an anti-Simon protest at Lahore, veteran nationalist *Lala Lajpat Rai* was brutally assaulted in a police lathi charge ordered by British officer James Scott. Lajpat Rai succumbed to his injuries, famously declaring: *"Every blow struck on my chest will be a nail in the coffin of the British Empire."*
- *The Nehru Report (1928):* Drafted by an all-parties committee chaired by Motilal Nehru, it proposed a Dominion Status constitution with joint electorates and fundamental rights, which was rejected by the Muslim League led by M.A. Jinnah (who issued his "Fourteen Points").

== The Lahore Congress and the Purna Swaraj Pledge (1929)
In December 1929, the historic annual session of the Congress convened on the banks of the Ravi River in Lahore under the charismatic presidency of the young *Jawaharlal Nehru*:
- *The Purna Swaraj Resolution:* The Congress officially declared its goal to be *Purna Swaraj* (Complete Independence), abandoning the demand for Dominion Status.
- *Unfurling the Flag:* At midnight on *31 December 1929*, Nehru unfurled the newly adopted tricolor national flag of freedom amidst jubilant celebrations.
- *First Independence Day (26 January 1930):* The Congress Working Committee decided that *26 January 1930* would be observed nationwide as *Independence Day*, with millions taking a solemn pledge affirming the inalienable right of the Indian people to overthrow an oppressive colonial regime that had ruined them economically, politically, culturally, and spiritually.

