= 5. Civil Disobedience, The Salt March & Constitutional Pacts (1930–1934)

To inaugurate the second great pan-Indian mass movement, Gandhi selected a simple, universal everyday commodity: *salt*:

== The Salt Satyagraha and the Dandi March (1930)
Under British law, the colonial state possessed an exclusive monopoly over the manufacture and sale of salt, imposing a regressive excise tax that fell with equal burden upon the poorest peasant and the wealthiest landlord:
- *The Strategic Genius of Salt:* Salt was a universal human necessity, cutting across all caste, religious, and class divides. Gandhi recognized that an assault on the salt monopoly would emotionally unify the entire nation.
- *The March:* On *12 March 1930*, Gandhi departed from the Sabarmati Ashram in Ahmedabad with *78 chosen ashramites* on a 240-mile march to the coastal village of *Dandi* on the Arabian Sea coast of Gujarat.
- *Dandi Defiance (6 April 1930):* Walking 10 miles a day over 24 days, Gandhi reached Dandi on 5 April. On the morning of *6 April 1930*, after ritual morning prayers and a ceremonial sea bath, Gandhi picked up a lump of natural salt mud from the beach, declaring: *"With this, I am shaking the foundations of the British Empire!"*

== Pan-Indian Spread of Civil Disobedience
The violation of the salt laws sparked widespread civil disobedience across the country:
- *Tamil Nadu:* *C. Rajagopalachari* led a salt march from Tiruchirappalli to *Vedaranyam* on the Tanjore coast.
- *Malabar (Kerala):* *K. Kelappan* marched from Calicut to *Payyanur*.
- *North-West Frontier Province (NWFP):* *Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan* (popularly known as the *"Frontier Gandhi"*) mobilized the Pathans through his non-violent volunteer organization, the *Khudai Khidmatgars* ("Servants of God" or "Red Shirts"). When British troops ordered the Garhwal Rifles to fire on an unarmed crowd at Peshawar, the soldiers refused to shoot their fellow countrymen!
- *Dharasana Salt Raid:* In Gujarat, poet *Sarojini Naidu*, accompanied by Gandhi's son Manilal, led 2,500 peaceful satyagrahis to non-violently raid the government Dharasana Salt Works. American journalist *Webb Miller* documented the horrifying brutality of British police, who beat unarmed satyagrahis on their heads with steel-tipped lathis while the victims stoically collapsed without raising a hand to defend themselves. Miller's syndicated reports flashed around the world, turning global public opinion decisively against British rule.

== The Round Table Conferences and the Poona Pact
- *The First RTC (1930):* Held in London; boycotted by the Congress; British authorities realized that constitutional settlements were impossible without Congress participation.
- *The Gandhi-Irwin Pact (5 March 1931):* Viceroy Lord Irwin and Gandhi negotiated as diplomatic equals:
  - Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and participate in the Second RTC.
  - Government agreed to release all non-violent political prisoners, return confiscated lands, and permit coastal villagers to manufacture salt for personal consumption.
- *The Second RTC (London, late 1931):* Gandhi sailed to London as the *sole official representative* of the Congress. The conference collapsed because the British government and sectional leaders (including the Muslim League and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar) prioritized separate communal electorates over national independence. Disillusioned, Gandhi returned to India and resumed Civil Disobedience.
- *The Poona Pact (September 1932):* In August 1932, British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald announced the *Communal Award*, which granted *separate electorates* to the "Depressed Classes" (Dalits). Gandhi viewed this as an imperial conspiracy to permanently divide Hindu society. He undertook a *fast unto death* in Yerwada Central Jail in Poona. On *24 September 1932*, Gandhi and Dr. *B.R. Ambedkar* reached an agreement known as the *Poona Pact*: separate electorates were abandoned in exchange for a substantial increase in *reserved seats* (from 71 to 148) in general provincial legislatures.

= 6. World War II, Cripps Mission & The Quit India Movement (1939–1945)

The outbreak of the Second World War in September 1939 plunged India into an unprecedented political storm:

== The Resignation of Congress Ministries (1939)
Without consulting Indian political leaders or the elected provincial legislatures, Viceroy Lord Linlithgow unilaterally declared India to be at war with Nazi Germany. In protest against this imperial autocracy, Congress ministries in all seven provinces resigned en masse in October and November 1939.

== The Cripps Mission (March 1942)
Faced with advancing Japanese forces conquering Southeast Asia and threatening India's eastern borders, the British War Cabinet under Winston Churchill dispatched Sir Stafford Cripps to Delhi in March 1942:
- *Cripps' Offer:* Post-war Dominion Status, a constituent assembly, and a clause permitting provinces to secede from the Indian Union.
- *Rejection:* Gandhi dismissed the offer as a *"post-dated cheque on a crashing bank"*, because the British refused to concede immediate transfer of real executive and defense power to Indian hands.

== The Quit India Movement (August 1942)
With the failure of the Cripps Mission and the threat of imminent Japanese invasion, Gandhi resolved that only immediate independence could mobilize the Indian people:
- *The Bombay Resolution (8 August 1942):* The All India Congress Committee met at the Gowalia Tank Maidan in Bombay and ratified the historic *Quit India Resolution*.
- *The "Do or Die" Mantra:* Gandhi delivered his electric speech: *"Here is a mantra, a short one, that I give you. The mantra is: 'Do or Die' (Karo ya Maro). We shall either free India or die in the attempt; we shall not live to see the perpetuation of our slavery."*
- *Operation Zero Hour:* Before dawn on *9 August 1942*, British police launched a preemptive strike, arresting Gandhi, Nehru, Patel, Azad, and every major member of the Congress Working Committee under the Defense of India Rules.
- *Spontaneous Mass Upsurge:* Leaderless, the Indian masses rose in an unprecedented insurrection. Young activists, students, and peasants attacked railways, cut telegraph lines, torched police stations, and hoisted the tricolor on government buildings.
- *Underground Networks:* Leaders like *Jayaprakash Narayan*, *Rammanohar Lohia*, *Aruna Asaf Ali*, and *Usha Mehta* operated an underground resistance network. Usha Mehta operated the clandestine *Secret Congress Radio*, broadcasting news of the freedom struggle across western India until she was arrested.
- *Parallel Governments (*Prati Sarkar*):*
  - *Ballia (UP):* Under Chittu Pandey, rebels took over the district administration for several days.
  - *Tamluk (Midnapore, Bengal):* The *Jatiya Sarkar* established local courts and organized the *Bidyut Bahini* volunteer force.
  - *Satara (Maharashtra):* The longest-lasting parallel government (*Prati Sarkar*), organized by Nana Patil and Y.B. Chavan, established people's courts (*nyaydan mandals*) that functioned until 1944.

= 7. Partition, Independence, and the Mahatma's Martyrdom (1946–1948)

The post-war period witnessed the tragic confluence of impending freedom and catastrophic communal partition:

== The Cabinet Mission and Direct Action Day (1946)
In 1946, the British Labour government sent the *Cabinet Mission* (Pethick-Lawrence, Stafford Cripps, and A.V. Alexander) to formulate a constitutional framework:
- *Three-Tier Confederation:* It proposed a united Indian confederation with three groups of provinces (Group A, B, and C).
- *Breakdown:* Disagreements over provincial grouping led to the breakdown of the plan. On *16 August 1946*, M.A. Jinnah called for *"Direct Action Day"* to enforce the demand for Pakistan. Horrific communal massacres erupted in Calcutta (*The Great Calcutta Killings*), which rapidly spread to Noakhali (East Bengal), Bihar, and the Punjab.

== The "One-Man Boundary Force" in Calcutta and Noakhali
While political leaders negotiated in New Delhi, the 77-year-old Mahatma walked barefoot through the violence-ravaged villages of Noakhali and Bihar, staying in Muslim homes and preaching non-violence:
- *15 August 1947:* When India celebrated independence at midnight, Gandhi was not in Delhi to hear Nehru's "Tryst with Destiny" address. He was in riot-torn Calcutta, fasting and spinning yarn at Beliaghata to stop communal bloodletting.
- *Mountbatten's Tribute:* Recognizing that Gandhi had succeeded in maintaining peace where thousands of armed soldiers had failed, Viceroy *Lord Mountbatten* praised him as the *"One-Man Boundary Force"*.

== Martyrdom (30 January 1948)
Returning to Delhi in late 1947, Gandhi fasted again to compel the Government of India to honor its financial obligation to transfer Rs 55 crore to Pakistan, and to demand peace for Delhi's Muslim citizens. Hindu extremists condemned his insistence on communal fairness as appeasement:
- *The Assassination:* On the evening of *30 January 1948*, as Gandhi walked to his daily prayer meeting at Birla House in New Delhi, a Hindu fanatic, *Nathuram Godse*, stepped forward, bowed, and shot him three times at point-blank range. As he fell, Gandhi uttered his final words: *"He Ram!"*
- *Nehru's Broadcast:* That night, Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru addressed a grief-stricken nation on All India Radio: *"Friends and comrades, the light has gone out of our lives and there is darkness everywhere... Our beloved leader, Bapu as we called him, the father of the nation, is no more."*

